<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bodawpaya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1745">1745</a>–<a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bodawpaya was the sixth king of the Konbaung dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, reigning from 1782 to 1819. He conquered the kingdom of Arakan in 1784 to 1785, carrying the revered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahamuni-buddha">Mahamuni Buddha</a> image to his capital, and made costly failed invasions of Siam. His annexations brought Burma into contact with British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, setting the stage for the Anglo-Burmese wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bodawpaya.jpg" alt="Bodawpaya" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kantabon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statute_of_king_Bodawpaya_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Maung Shwe Waing and later known as Badon Min, he was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a>, founder of the Konbaung dynasty. He became king by deposing his nephew Phaungkaza Maung Maung, son of his eldest brother Naungdawgyi, at Ava, and in 1782 moved the royal capital back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>. His title was Hsinbyumyashin, Lord of the White Elephants, but posterity knows him as Bodawpaya, the Grandsire, in relation to his grandson and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a>. He fathered 70 sons and 67 daughters by about 54 consorts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1784 his son, the Heir Apparent Thado Minsaw, led the royal armies across the Western Yoma into Arakan and took its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a> on the last day of the year. Besides the Mahamuni image and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> bronze statues, 20,000 captives were carried off as slaves to pagodas and temples. The Arakanese revolted in 1794, and the Governor of India Sir John Shore sent Captain Michael Symes on an embassy to Ava. A revolt by the governor of Tavoy in 1791, backed by Siam, ended in 1793 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> coast in Burmese hands. A second invasion of Siam in 1809 was repelled, and with it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> lost Lan Na after some 200 years of rule.</p><p>In 1816 the Ahom governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badan-chandra-borphukan">Badan Chandra Borphukan</a>, asked for help against his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnananda-burhagohain">Purnananda Burhagohain</a>, and a Burmese force of 16,000 entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in January 1817 and won the battle of Ghiladhari. The Ahom princess Hemo Aideo was given to Bodawpaya in marriage. When Borphukan was assassinated and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrakanta-singha">Chandrakanta Singha</a> deposed, a second Burmese invasion in February 1819 restored him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bodawpaya proclaimed himself the coming Buddha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a>, a claim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a> firmly rejected. He set up a chapter of senior monks to guard the order's discipline and unified the monks under the Thudhamma order, and Burmese ordination was carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, where it founded the Amarapura Nikaya. In 1790 he began the Mantalagyi stupa at Mingun, meant to stand 150 metres high but left unfinished, and the 90-ton Mingun Bell was cast for it between 1808 and 1810. He ordered an economic survey of the kingdom in 1784. He died in 1819 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a>, his son the Heir Apparent having died in 1808.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bodawpaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His sons Naungdawgyi, Hsinbyushin and later <b>Bodawpaya</b> extended the realm into the most powerful state in mainland Southeast Asia of its time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1785 <b>Bodawpaya</b> of the Konbaung dynasty invaded along a broad front in what Thai histories call the Nine Armies War; the Siamese defeated the main thrust at Lat Ya near Kanchanaburi, the attack on Thalang on Phuket island was repulsed, and later Burmese campaigns, the last major…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The vaulting techniques of the Bagan era were lost in later periods: in the 18th century King <b>Bodawpaya</b> tried to build the Mingun Pagoda as a spacious vaulted temple but failed, as the craftsmen and masons of his time had lost the knowledge of vaulting and keystone arching.</blockquote><a href="/work/bagan">Bagan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1784 to 1785 the Konbaung dynasty under <b>Bodawpaya</b> conquered Arakan and carried off the Mahamuni Buddha.</blockquote><a href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Mrauk U Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Shwebo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.57,95.7&amp;year=1793" aria-label="Shwebo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bodawpaya"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5449 5623h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5449 5623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.57,95.7&amp;year=1793">What was here in 1793? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
